WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.360
Speaker 1: Well, my friends, Joe Biden is doing&nbsp;
it. Joe Biden will be canceling up to $20,000 in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:15.360
student loan debt for those making under $125,000.&nbsp;
It'll be $10,000 for most folks. But there's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:20.320
Pell Grant element to it which could raise that&nbsp;
to $20,000. We're going to look at the details.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.360
And I have to tell you, producer Pat was&nbsp;
completely correct in his prediction. Producer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:33.760
Pat has been predicting on the award winning bonus&nbsp;
show that at a time advantageous to Democrats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:39.120
for the midterm elections, Joe Biden would&nbsp;
do some larger student loan forgiveness than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:44.320
he has done so far. Now, remember that Joe&nbsp;
Biden has already done even before this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:50.400
more student loan forgiveness than any other&nbsp;
president. And we've covered that before. And Pat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:56.080
said it's going to be close ish to the November&nbsp;
midterms. Joe Biden's going to do something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.080 --> 00:01:01.680
And I just didn't think he was. And here we are.&nbsp;
Producer Pat, completely correct. New York Times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:08.800
reporting Biden to cancel $10,000 in student&nbsp;
debt. Low income students are eligible for more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:17.360
The plan would be $10,000 in cancelation&nbsp;
for those earning under $125,000 a year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:24.080
and $20,000 for those who received Pell Grants for&nbsp;
low income families. Now, the debt forgiveness is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:30.400
less than what some Democrats have been pushing&nbsp;
for, and we will talk about that. Biden also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:36.720
announced that a pandemic era pause on student&nbsp;
loan payments, which has been in effect since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:43.920
March of 2020, will expire at the end of the&nbsp;
year. There is not yet an application process.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:49.840
This has been a big question. Okay. So how do&nbsp;
how do we get the forgiveness if we qualify?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:57.280
What's the process? That process has not yet been&nbsp;
made specific. A couple of other important notes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:03.040
here. Students who receive Pell Grants will be&nbsp;
able to get up to 20,000 in forgiveness. About&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:09.680
60% of borrowers have Pell Grants and the majority&nbsp;
come from families earning under $30,000 a year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:15.440
The Education Department estimates 27 million&nbsp;
borrowers will qualify for up to 20,000 in relief.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:23.120
Then you've got millions more who will be able to&nbsp;
get the 10,000. And there are a number of other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:29.360
repayment modifications that are being made as&nbsp;
well. So let's go through some of the different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.800
reactions to this and then we're going to&nbsp;
delve further into it. So one reaction is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:41.680
this is not enough. 10,000 is not enough. 20,000&nbsp;
is not enough. Fine, fine. But this is already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.440
overwhelmingly more than any other president&nbsp;
has done by far. So as I've said before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.600
I tend to evaluate presidents based&nbsp;
on what they do, generally speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:03:01.280
and also for things they promised to do but don't&nbsp;
do. I think both are fair game just saying, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:06.320
this president's bad because he didn't do these&nbsp;
ten things that he never promised to do. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:12.640
seems to be a way to end up with relatively&nbsp;
meaningless evaluations of presidents. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:20.000
when you look at what Joe Biden has done, he has&nbsp;
by far forgiven more student debt than any other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:26.000
president. Counterpoint Joe Biden is only doing&nbsp;
this to help Democrats in November. Well, I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.680
those and everything politicians do to some degree&nbsp;
is to help themselves. Is it also good for people?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:37.280
And the answer is this is very good for people.&nbsp;
And there are also some new rules for income based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:44.000
repayment plans, which are arguably as big of a&nbsp;
deal as the forgiveness repayment will be capped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:50.960
at 5% of discretionary income rather than ten.&nbsp;
That's a big source of relief. Your payments are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:59.360
zero if you are below 225% of the federal poverty&nbsp;
line due to an increase in the non-discretionary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:05.760
fraction of income, unpaid interest is waived&nbsp;
as long as you're making monthly payments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:12.960
even if the payments are zero. Based on those&nbsp;
earlier guidelines that I mentioned, and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:21.920
really a game changer. Many people are much more&nbsp;
able to pay off a debt if they're not accumulating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:28.080
and accruing more and more and more interest&nbsp;
through the payment process. And that's a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:35.680
big deal. And then forgiveness after ten years&nbsp;
on loans under $12,000 rather than only $20,000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:43.440
which is another interesting provision. So&nbsp;
there is a significant amount of relief here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:49.200
in addition to the forgiveness component for lower&nbsp;
and middle income borrowers. And that's a really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:55.120
really big deal. Now, one question is, what&nbsp;
does this do in terms of Democrat chances&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:01.440
going into the November midterms as we have&nbsp;
talked? About before. Right now in the House&nbsp;&nbsp;

